WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:03.200
Now that we understand a little bit

2
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:07.000
about how the material we are creating is going to function,

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.240
let's go ahead and start creating that material.

4
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.199
Now to do this, we're first gonna have to make

5
00:00:11.199 --> 00:00:13.960
a brand new material inside of our content browser.

6
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.339
And then when we open that material up,

7
00:00:16.339 --> 00:00:18.640
we're gonna add two texture samples

8
00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.800
and we're gonna convert these to texture parameters,

9
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.399
one called BC Dirt and the other called BC Grass.

10
00:00:25.399 --> 00:00:26.640
And the reason that we're creating these

11
00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.120
as texture parameters is to make it easier

12
00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.079
for us to adjust the landscapes textures

13
00:00:32.079 --> 00:00:36.039
once the landscape has been created and turned into an instant.

14
00:00:36.039 --> 00:00:38.960
So let's go ahead and pop over to the editor now.

15
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:41.079
With the editor open, I'm gonna hit Control Space

16
00:00:41.079 --> 00:00:42.439
and I'm just gonna come here

17
00:00:42.439 --> 00:00:45.960
and I'm going to right click, go to my material

18
00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:51.630
and just do MAT underscore landscape.

19
00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:54.009
And then we're gonna double click on this to open it up.

20
00:00:54.009 --> 00:00:56.270
Now the texture samples that we use

21
00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:58.229
are not really that important

22
00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:00.990
because remember, we're gonna make these into parameters.

23
00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:03.829
So let's go ahead and just pull in two textures.

24
00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:06.030
Now we can do this a number of different ways.

25
00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:08.629
The way that I like to do it is hold down the T key

26
00:01:08.629 --> 00:01:09.909
on the keyboard and click

27
00:01:09.909 --> 00:01:11.469
and that's gonna add a texture sample.

28
00:01:11.469 --> 00:01:14.069
Now, unfortunately, this just adds this.

29
00:01:14.069 --> 00:01:17.329
We do need to add some type of texture inside of here.

30
00:01:17.329 --> 00:01:18.670
The other way that we can do this

31
00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:20.049
is if we hit Control Space

32
00:01:20.049 --> 00:01:22.109
and we come over here to say like surfaces,

33
00:01:22.109 --> 00:01:23.629
I'm gonna go up here to let my dried grass

34
00:01:23.629 --> 00:01:26.230
and I just grabbed this texture and pull it in like so.

35
00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:29.430
It's also going to pull that texture in.

36
00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:32.709
So I'm gonna actually just delete this one

37
00:01:32.709 --> 00:01:34.750
and we're gonna right click on this for the time being.

38
00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:36.950
I'm gonna go convert to parameter

39
00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:39.349
and we're gonna call this one BC for base color

40
00:01:39.349 --> 00:01:41.980
underscore grass.

41
00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:43.620
And then I'm gonna hit Control Space

42
00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.180
and we're gonna go to,

43
00:01:46.299 --> 00:01:47.980
we'll go to this rocky ground right here

44
00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:50.299
and we'll grab this texture sampler.

45
00:01:50.299 --> 00:01:53.780
We'll pull that in and then we're gonna right click

46
00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:58.780
and go convert to parameter BC underscore dirt.

47
00:02:01.409 --> 00:02:05.170
All right, so next what we're going to do

48
00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:10.169
is we are going to create our slope setup.

49
00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:12.530
Now this involves the following nodes.

50
00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:14.650
We have a slope angle.

51
00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:17.569
The slope angle allows us to control the angle

52
00:02:17.569 --> 00:02:20.530
at which this interaction is going to happen.

53
00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:22.250
So generally we want this to happen

54
00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:23.770
from the top to the bottom,

55
00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:24.930
but there could be an instance

56
00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:26.490
where maybe we want some action

57
00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:28.050
from the left side or the right side.

58
00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.050
So we're gonna add this in there

59
00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:32.729
just in case we wanna mess around

60
00:02:32.729 --> 00:02:34.969
with these values a little bit and make some adjustments.

61
00:02:34.969 --> 00:02:37.370
We then have the slope fall off power

62
00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:38.650
and the slope contrast.

63
00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:40.669
Now what these two nodes allow us to do

64
00:02:40.669 --> 00:02:44.810
is control the way our two textures

65
00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:46.889
are going to be mixed.

66
00:02:46.889 --> 00:02:49.289
The slope fall off power is gonna control

67
00:02:49.289 --> 00:02:52.789
kind of the fall off between texture A and texture B

68
00:02:52.789 --> 00:02:55.150
and the contrast is going to increase

69
00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:57.430
or decrease the sharpness of that.

70
00:02:57.430 --> 00:02:59.629
Finally, all of these nodes are gonna be plugged

71
00:02:59.629 --> 00:03:03.710
into our slope mask, which is a material function

72
00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:08.710
that allows us to define the slope part A

73
00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:10.550
and the slope part B.

74
00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:15.759
So let's go ahead and set this up inside of our material.

75
00:03:15.759 --> 00:03:18.240
So we're gonna pop over here to the material editor.

76
00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.479
I'm just gonna pull back here a little bit

77
00:03:20.479 --> 00:03:23.000
and we are going to first off,

78
00:03:23.159 --> 00:03:25.599
we're gonna hold down the four key on the keyboard

79
00:03:25.599 --> 00:03:27.919
and left click and that's gonna add

80
00:03:27.919 --> 00:03:31.180
a four channel parameter node,

81
00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:32.580
which we can then right click on

82
00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:35.020
and convert to a parameter.

83
00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:42.300
And we're gonna call this slope angle

84
00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:43.780
and then the default value here

85
00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:47.620
is going to be one in the blue.

86
00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:49.039
All right, so next what we need to do

87
00:03:49.039 --> 00:03:51.400
is we need to add some constant parameter nodes.

88
00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:54.879
So I'm gonna hold down the one key and click once

89
00:03:54.879 --> 00:03:57.039
and that's gonna add a constant parameter.

90
00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:59.599
So again, holding down the one key on the keyboard

91
00:03:59.599 --> 00:04:02.759
and then left clicking will add that constant parameter.

92
00:04:02.759 --> 00:04:05.639
Then we're gonna click on this, right click on it

93
00:04:05.639 --> 00:04:07.879
and convert to a parameter.

94
00:04:07.879 --> 00:04:09.800
And we are gonna call this first one.

95
00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:15.139
This should be slope fall off power.

96
00:04:15.139 --> 00:04:17.600
And we're gonna give it a default value of one.

97
00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.339
And then what I'm gonna do is I'm gonna hit Control C

98
00:04:20.339 --> 00:04:23.699
and then Control V, whoops, Control C, Control V.

99
00:04:23.699 --> 00:04:26.199
That's gonna duplicate this node for us.

100
00:04:26.199 --> 00:04:28.759
And we're gonna call this new node that we duplicated

101
00:04:28.759 --> 00:04:31.040
slope contrast.

102
00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:34.850
And we're gonna leave its value set to 1.0.

103
00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:36.550
Next, what we need to do is we need to add

104
00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:37.470
our slope mask.

105
00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.189
Now, unfortunately, we can't add the slope mask

106
00:04:39.189 --> 00:04:40.430
through a keyboard shortcut.

107
00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:42.430
We do need to click inside of here

108
00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:45.910
and then search for our slope.

109
00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:48.350
And right here, we have a slope mask.

110
00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:49.750
It's just showing up in two locations

111
00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:50.850
so we can select either or.

112
00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:53.139
So I'm gonna select this top one right here.

113
00:04:53.139 --> 00:04:55.459
So there we go, there's our slope mask.

114
00:04:55.459 --> 00:05:00.370
And what we're gonna do next is we are gonna connect

115
00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:02.350
everything together like this.

116
00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:07.350
So we're going to go ahead and set up our BC dirt

117
00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:10.689
and our BC grass, and then we're gonna use a lerp.

118
00:05:10.689 --> 00:05:13.410
And a lerp stands for linear interpolate.

119
00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:17.649
And lerps are really a fantastic thing

120
00:05:17.649 --> 00:05:20.649
inside of your materials as they allow you to mix

121
00:05:20.649 --> 00:05:25.649
two inputs like our BC dirt and our BC grass by an alpha.

122
00:05:25.889 --> 00:05:29.209
And this is really where things start to happen.

123
00:05:29.209 --> 00:05:31.050
So with this setup on screen,

124
00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:32.610
which we're gonna make in just a second,

125
00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:36.050
this will already allow us to have some type

126
00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:37.889
of interaction with our landscape.

127
00:05:37.889 --> 00:05:39.209
So let's go ahead and set this up now.

128
00:05:39.209 --> 00:05:44.810
So back over in the editor, we are going to,

129
00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:46.949
first, we're gonna add our lerp parameter.

130
00:05:46.949 --> 00:05:50.029
So to add a lerp, we can hold the L key down

131
00:05:50.029 --> 00:05:52.730
on the keyboard and press, and that's gonna add our lerp.

132
00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:56.250
So we're gonna connect the RGB or the RGB output

133
00:05:57.129 --> 00:05:58.689
of our BC grass into the A input,

134
00:05:58.689 --> 00:06:02.089
and then the RGB of our BC dirt into the B.

135
00:06:02.089 --> 00:06:04.529
And now what we need to do is just set this up.

136
00:06:04.529 --> 00:06:07.329
Now this is actually pretty easy to set up.

137
00:06:07.329 --> 00:06:11.529
All we need to do is take the output of our slope angle

138
00:06:11.529 --> 00:06:12.990
and plug that into here.

139
00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:16.089
And then I wanna move these two parameters down like slow.

140
00:06:16.089 --> 00:06:20.730
So, and we have our slope fall off power,

141
00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:22.730
which is going to be right up here.

142
00:06:22.730 --> 00:06:24.769
And then this cheap contrast is gonna go

143
00:06:24.769 --> 00:06:27.319
in our slope contrast.

144
00:06:27.319 --> 00:06:28.980
Once all of that has been completed,

145
00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:30.959
all we need to do now is take this result

146
00:06:30.959 --> 00:06:33.399
and plug it right here into our alpha,

147
00:06:33.399 --> 00:06:35.360
and then take the output of our alpha

148
00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:37.879
and plug it into our base color.

149
00:06:37.879 --> 00:06:38.959
Now that we have that done,

150
00:06:38.959 --> 00:06:41.180
we can actually see this kind of happening in action.

151
00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:43.759
We can see there is our dirt

152
00:06:43.759 --> 00:06:45.660
and then there is our grass.

153
00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:48.120
So let's go ahead and apply and save this

154
00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:50.519
so we can save our work.

155
00:06:50.519 --> 00:06:52.199
Now I'm gonna move some things around here

156
00:06:52.199 --> 00:06:55.399
because the next thing that I want to talk about

157
00:06:55.399 --> 00:06:58.459
is the named reroute.

158
00:06:58.459 --> 00:07:01.600
Now, as this material starts to grow in size,

159
00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:04.600
it is gonna become very difficult to read

160
00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:07.620
through the various wires that connect everything.

161
00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:12.620
So we can add this new named reroute declaration node.

162
00:07:12.699 --> 00:07:14.019
And this is really cool

163
00:07:14.019 --> 00:07:15.560
because what this allows us to do

164
00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:19.079
is it allows us to put blocks of shader code

165
00:07:19.079 --> 00:07:20.279
off to the side.

166
00:07:20.279 --> 00:07:22.879
And then instead of having to make a wire connection

167
00:07:22.879 --> 00:07:24.439
across the entire graph,

168
00:07:24.439 --> 00:07:27.759
we can pull these little named reroute declaration nodes

169
00:07:27.759 --> 00:07:30.439
out, plug them into their respective inputs,

170
00:07:30.439 --> 00:07:33.360
and that will make that connection with us

171
00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:36.199
without us having to really worry too much

172
00:07:36.199 --> 00:07:38.000
about the wire interaction.

173
00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.800
So let's go ahead and pull one of these out

174
00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:42.420
by coming over here to the editor,

175
00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:44.459
and we're gonna right-click inside of here

176
00:07:44.459 --> 00:07:47.060
and look for a named, and right there,

177
00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:50.079
add a named reroute declaration node.

178
00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:51.879
So we're gonna put this in here,

179
00:07:51.879 --> 00:07:58.139
and we're gonna call this new one slope.

180
00:07:58.139 --> 00:08:00.600
And let's take that.

181
00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:01.920
So slope, there we go.

182
00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:03.399
And then what we're gonna do

183
00:08:03.399 --> 00:08:06.079
is I'm actually gonna hold down Alt and click right here,

184
00:08:06.079 --> 00:08:07.720
which is gonna break our connection.

185
00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:10.040
And we're gonna move this little bit of shader logic

186
00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:11.829
just over here.

187
00:08:11.829 --> 00:08:13.990
And then for these named reroutes to work,

188
00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:15.430
what we do is we take this result

189
00:08:15.430 --> 00:08:17.920
and we just plug it in right there like that.

190
00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:19.399
Then we come over here,

191
00:08:19.399 --> 00:08:23.839
and if we just click inside of the shader graph,

192
00:08:23.839 --> 00:08:26.399
we look for this named reroutes, we grab the slope,

193
00:08:26.399 --> 00:08:28.639
and then we plug this in like so.

194
00:08:28.639 --> 00:08:31.139
So look, we have the exact same behavior

195
00:08:31.139 --> 00:08:32.080
that we had before.

196
00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:33.879
And if I just undo this, we can see here,

197
00:08:33.879 --> 00:08:35.620
there is a little bit,

198
00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:38.080
but it's kind of mixing it all together.

199
00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:40.039
So it's just showing kind of both of them.

200
00:08:40.039 --> 00:08:41.399
If we plug this back in,

201
00:08:41.399 --> 00:08:44.559
you can see here it's got the rock and then the grass.

202
00:08:44.559 --> 00:08:47.590
So let's go ahead and apply and save.

203
00:08:47.590 --> 00:08:49.830
And this concept, and here's again,

204
00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:52.230
an example of what we did, if you need to catch up,

205
00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:54.970
we took our slope mask, we took our slope angle,

206
00:08:54.970 --> 00:08:56.590
we put that into our fall off power

207
00:08:56.590 --> 00:08:57.629
and our cheap contrast,

208
00:08:57.629 --> 00:09:02.250
and then we added our named declaration for slope.

209
00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:04.490
And then inside of our lerp,

210
00:09:04.490 --> 00:09:07.009
we just looked for our named reroute

211
00:09:07.009 --> 00:09:09.090
and we input that into our alpha.

212
00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:10.809
Now this is really, really powerful

213
00:09:10.809 --> 00:09:12.090
because we're gonna be doing this

214
00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:15.090
for a lot of other things that we need to do.

215
00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:16.429
For example, next up,

216
00:09:16.429 --> 00:09:18.850
we are going to add some tiling,

217
00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:22.610
but in order to do this for all of the different things

218
00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:23.950
that we're gonna have in this,

219
00:09:23.950 --> 00:09:27.330
we are going to make our tiling code off to the side,

220
00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:28.909
like with our slope contrast,

221
00:09:28.909 --> 00:09:31.230
and then we're gonna add a named reroute.

222
00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:33.940
So let's go ahead and set this up now.

223
00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:36.259
All right, so first off, we're gonna come over here

224
00:09:36.259 --> 00:09:42.309
and we're gonna add a texture coordinate.

225
00:09:42.309 --> 00:09:44.590
So there's our texture coordinate.

226
00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:46.769
Then I'm gonna hold down the one key and click,

227
00:09:46.809 --> 00:09:49.440
and that's gonna add a constant,

228
00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:52.360
which we're gonna right click and convert to a parameter,

229
00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:59.759
which we are going to call grass underscore tile.

230
00:10:00.879 --> 00:10:05.190
We're gonna give this a default value of one.

231
00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:08.350
Then what we're gonna do is I'm gonna hold down

232
00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:13.639
the M key on the keyboard and click to add a multiply.

233
00:10:13.639 --> 00:10:17.179
And then we're gonna connect like so.

234
00:10:17.179 --> 00:10:19.279
Now, once we have these two connected,

235
00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:22.440
we are going to look for a,

236
00:10:25.019 --> 00:10:28.690
add a named reroute declaration node.

237
00:10:28.690 --> 00:10:33.820
And we are gonna call this grass underscore tile.

238
00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:39.169
Oh, and it looks like it didn't take the name again.

239
00:10:39.169 --> 00:10:42.110
And we're gonna plug our output of our multiply

240
00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:45.970
into our grass tile.

241
00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:48.169
So let's just recap and give everybody a chance

242
00:10:48.169 --> 00:10:49.009
to catch up.

243
00:10:49.009 --> 00:10:50.429
Here is what we've been doing.

244
00:10:50.429 --> 00:10:53.570
So there is our grass tile with our texture cord.

245
00:10:53.570 --> 00:10:54.409
That's multiply.

246
00:10:54.409 --> 00:10:56.370
Remember, we got that multiply by pressing M

247
00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:57.889
on the keyboard and left clicking.

248
00:10:57.889 --> 00:11:02.659
And then we added our named reroute declaration,

249
00:11:02.659 --> 00:11:06.559
which we called grass tile.

250
00:11:06.559 --> 00:11:09.220
Now, the next thing that we're going to do

251
00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:11.320
is we're gonna duplicate this work,

252
00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:14.639
but we're gonna call this new one dirt tile.

253
00:11:14.639 --> 00:11:16.320
And then once we've created that dirt tile,

254
00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:20.799
we're gonna plug in our grass tile

255
00:11:20.799 --> 00:11:23.019
to match what we see on the screen in front of us.

256
00:11:23.019 --> 00:11:24.559
So let's pop over to the editor now

257
00:11:24.559 --> 00:11:25.759
and go ahead and take care of that.

258
00:11:25.759 --> 00:11:28.379
So really easily, we can come up here

259
00:11:28.419 --> 00:11:31.580
and we can right click and just duplicate this

260
00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:33.080
and move it up.

261
00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:35.779
And now we need to just change this from grass to dirt.

262
